Finding: {"title":"Two parallel, inconsistent standby-request implementations for students","severity":"LOW","area":"Architecture / Duplication","files":["src/components/features/mvp-v2/pages/student-standby.tsx","src/components/features/mvp-v2/components/student-standby-panel.tsx"],"detail":"`student-standby.tsx` (page at presumably /standby) talks directly to `/locations/:locationId/schedule/standby` (backed by `routes-standby.ts` / `scheduleStandbyRepository`, a separate 'notify first-in-queue' waitlist keyed by date/time slot). `student-standby-panel.tsx` (a drawer component) talks to a completely different service (`../services/standby` — `fetchStandbyOpportunities`/`joinStandby`/`leaveStandby`), which appears to target actual `scheduleEvents` rows with an `approvalStatus` field (`auto_approved`/`pending_review`/`rejected`) — a different data model than `StandbyEntry` in the page file (`status: waiting/notified/confirmed/expired/cancelled`). These look like two independently built standby subsystems with different backing tables/statuses, not a shared component reused across two entry points.","impact":"Feature drift risk — a student using one entry point (drawer vs. full page) sees different standby state/behavior than the other; future bug fixes applied to one implementation won't propagate to the other, and it's unclear from the code which is the current source of truth.","recommendation":"Confirm with product/architecture whether both standby systems are intentionally distinct (e.g. one for schedule-event-based standby, one for date/time-slot waitlist) — if not, consolidate onto one data model and one set of routes.","confidence":"low"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

